 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>Set in a desecrated church, Galib Gassanoff’s Institution show had the solemnity and monumentality that have become part of his grammar of form. But this time something had shifted. The architecture of his clothes remained—structured, padded, squared—yet the weight seemed redistributed. More movement, more light, and a newfound sensuality lay beneath the ritualistic severity.</p><p>The starting point was the kelaghayi, the traditional Azerbaijani women’s silk scarf: a hand-stamped, dyed and re-dyed square, with a symbolism rooted in the culture of Gassanoff’s homeland. Rather than cutting it, he kept each scarf whole, folding, draping, and stitching it into garments while preserving its raw edges. The square remained a square; it simply changed state.</p><p>That idea traveled through the collection. Trench coats, leather jackets, and wool tailoring incorporated folds and layered constructions; flat sleeves were applied over sleeves, apron-like panels appeared on trousers, and topstitching traced construction across the surface. Shoulders dropped, volumes widened, and sculptural shapes were allowed to fall rather than hold themselves rigidly. Lighter fabrics, including georgette, introduced movement and revealed the body: Square pieces were joined into tunics with open backs, bringing a more instinctive sensuality to a vocabulary previously defined by protection and restraint.</p><p>The tension between hardness and softness was particularly effective. Cracked leather was treated, opened, and dyed by hand, while wool, bouclé, and padded tailoring were balanced by lighter, more fluid materials. Even the new shoes followed Gassanoff’s instinct for exaggeration, their broad, organic fronts sculpted with padding and made by hand.</p><p>Then came red. Usually operating within a disciplined black-and-white palette, Gassanoff introduced the color almost as a punctuation mark, saying simply that he wanted the collection to be “a little brighter and more interesting to the eye.” It also carries particular cultural resonance: Traditionally, Azerbaijani women wore red rather than white for marriage. Here, though, it was less a declaration than a spark, the smallest disruption to an otherwise stark palette.</p><p>Daş, the collection’s title, means stone in Azerbaijani, but also suggests something less fixed than stone itself: A surface can crack, something solid can yield. That seemed to describe the evolution of Gassanoff’s work rather well. His monumentality was still intact, but it has acquired fluidity; his structures still framed the body but no longer seemed determined to contain it. Even stone, Gassanoff seemed to remind us, can change state.</p></div>",
